WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.602
Every heap allocation needs a clear owner.

2
00:00:02.682 --> 00:00:06.243
The owner is the code responsible for
eventually releasing the object or

3
00:00:06.323 --> 00:00:08.133
transferring that responsibility.

4
00:00:08.213 --> 00:00:10.490
Without an owner, cleanup becomes
guesswork.

5
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.073
With multiple informal owners, release can
happen too early, too late,

6
00:00:14.153 --> 00:00:15.028
or more than once.

7
00:00:15.108 --> 00:00:17.269
C APIs often pass pointers in several
roles.

8
00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:21.085
A caller-owned pointer may be read or
modified without changing ownership.

9
00:00:21.165 --> 00:00:23.909
A callee-owned result may need to be freed
by the caller.

10
00:00:23.989 --> 00:00:26.441
A borrowed pointer may be valid only
during a call.

11
00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:29.323
An output parameter may receive newly
allocated memory.

12
00:00:29.403 --> 00:00:31.038
Each role needs a clear contract.

13
00:00:31.118 --> 00:00:33.979
Ownership transfer should be explicit at
API boundaries.

14
00:00:34.059 --> 00:00:36.219
Function names, comments, documentation,

15
00:00:36.299 --> 00:00:40.327
and parameter conventions should make it
obvious whether the caller must free the

16
00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:43.093
result, whether the callee consumes an
input pointer,

17
00:00:43.173 --> 00:00:45.099
or whether a pointer remains borrowed.

18
00:00:45.179 --> 00:00:47.573
Hidden ownership rules are difficult to
review.

19
00:00:47.653 --> 00:00:51.506
Returned heap objects and output
parameters are common sources of confusion.

20
00:00:51.586 --> 00:00:55.440
A function that fills an output pointer
should define what happens on success,

21
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.446
failure, and partial initialization.

22
00:00:57.526 --> 00:01:01.613
It should also define whether the output
is unchanged on failure or whether cleanup

23
00:01:01.693 --> 00:01:02.335
is required.

24
00:01:02.415 --> 00:01:06.560
Reviewers should be able to trace each
allocated object from allocation to release.

25
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.377
If that trace requires remembering private
conventions across many files,

26
00:01:10.457 --> 00:01:12.500
the API needs a clearer ownership design.
